From: =?UTF-8?q?=EC=8B=AC=EC=83=81=EC=9B=90?= <dev.chamomile@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 04:08:30 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] macos, windows: a FIFO for the log, metrics or serial output
 holds 64 KiB, as on Linux

Firecracker opens the log, metrics and serial output paths with
utils::open_file_nonblock, O_RDWR so that a FIFO never lacks a reader.
On Linux the FIFO then holds pipe(7)'s 65536 unread bytes. On macOS a
FIFO is a pair of local stream sockets with 8 KiB of buffer
(net.local.stream.sendspace), which no call on its descriptor raises.
On Windows the path names a pipe its reader made: it holds what the
reader gave it, and it ends with the reader, after which a write fails
(os error 232 or 233), where a FIFO held read-write takes it.

open_file_nonblock now returns wick's OutFile (overlay/vmm/ext/fifo.rs).
On Linux it is the File itself. On macOS a FIFO gets an overflow of 65536
less the FIFO's own buffer, moved into the FIFO as the reader makes room;
on Windows a pipe gets one of 65536 less what the reader's pipe holds,
which also takes what is written once the reader has gone, and is moved
into the pipe a reader makes again under the name. Anything else is
written directly.

The logger's target, FcLineWriter, both SerialOutInner::File and the
tests that build them from a File (through ext::fifo::plain) take an
OutFile. An orderly end drains the overflow first:
- upstream's panic hook, after its metrics;
- exit_with_code, before its _exit.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
